  "slug": "stochastic-gene-96d7",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and a published framework test how kinetic and stochastic models fit experimental data across molecular and cellular scales.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the use of reduced mechanistic models that are tested against experimental observations. The published framework compares replication-division models in E. coli and reports that a new double-process model fits data across growth conditions. A coarse-grained ODE model is paired with E. coli extract batch experiments to test mechanistic hypotheses about cell-free protein synthesis. Two preprints address molecular binding rather than cell physiology. One develops a coarse-grained stochastic model of nonspecific DNA-protein binding in the fast diffusion regime, including footprint size and conformational switching. The other shows, using stochastic simulations, that favourable mean hook-effect curves do not guarantee reliable complex formation in small systems. The distinctive feature is the use of stochastic and coarse-grained formulations to expose behaviour that mean-level descriptions may not capture.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
